From 4d5aa6490a97307e4306b1f040976a95c8f9ef17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:14:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 091/145] media: meson: vdec: do not demand every CAPTURE
 buffer be queued

Seeking froze the picture on the frame that was on screen while the
player's clock ran on at 1x, and once it happened every later seek was
dead too.  The decoder sat in STATUS_NEEDS_RESUME for good.

The CAPTURE queue is streamed on after the seek - vb2 logs

  [cap-...] vb2_core_streamon: successful

- but vdec_start_streaming() is never called for it, so codec_ops->
resume(), the only thing that reprograms the CAPTURE buffers, never
runs.  vb2_core_streamon() defers the driver callback:

	if (q->queued_count >= q->min_queued_buffers)
		ret = vb2_start_streaming(q);

and vdec_queue_setup() had set q->min_queued_buffers to num_dst_bufs,
i.e. *every* buffer.  Kodi's zero-copy DRMPRIME renderer keeps a couple
of frames on the display across a seek and re-queues the rest, so 18 of
20 arrive, the callback is deferred forever, and STREAMON silently
succeeds having started nothing.  How many frames the renderer happens
to be holding is what decided whether a given seek worked, which is why
this looked intermittent.

The all-buffers rule exists because codecs without a resume op program
the whole CAPTURE list once in start_streaming() and the firmware may
write into any of it.  Codecs that have a resume op do not work that
way - they (re)program buffers per frame - so require only the
interface minimum from them.

That means the per-buffer setup can no longer assume the queue holds
every buffer, so collect them by index from the vb2 queue instead of
walking the queued list.  An MMAP buffer has a valid DMA address from
allocation whether queued or not; an imported DMABUF is mapped only
while queued, so keep the queued-only walk for those.

Doing so exposes a latent bug the old rule had been hiding.  The
ANC2AXI table auto-increments from slot 0, and the reference lists
address it by vb2_buf.index (index * 2 and index * 2 + 1 for two-plane
formats), so it must be written densely, one slot per index - exactly
as the vendor driver writes it over MAX_REF_PIC_NUM.  Writing one slot
per queued buffer in queue order put every buffer after the first
absent index into the wrong slot.  With every buffer always queued the
two orders coincided; with a gap they do not, and the decoder would
reference the wrong frames.  Slots with no buffer behind them are
pointed at a real buffer so that a corrupt reference reads valid
memory rather than address zero.

Finally, refuse to decode into a CAPTURE buffer that has no FBC backing
(one queued late, or added by CREATE_BUFS): setup is per-index
idempotent, so retry it for that index, and drop the frame if it still
cannot be backed.  The fallback address for an unbacked index is zero,
and the hardware would otherwise write the frame over physical page 0.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
